---
.title = "Clipboard",
.description = "Read and write the system clipboard from Zig: text and PNG images, on every platform, from any thread.",
.date = .date("2026-10-12T00:00:00"),
.authors = ["Oriel"],
.layout = "docs.shtml",
---

`oriel.clipboard` reads and writes the system clipboard: plain text and
images (always as PNG bytes). It is the clipboard of the whole system, not
the page's: what it writes, every other app can paste, and it reads what
other apps copied, including while your window is in the background where
the platform allows it.

Use it when Zig needs the clipboard: a hotkey that rewrites the selected
text, a tray item that copies a value, a "copy" button whose content comes
from Zig. To put text into *another* app, write it here and send the paste
keystroke with [Input]($link.page('docs/input')). The API is Zig-only: the
page reaches it through one of your [commands]($link.page('docs/commands')).

# [Availability]($heading.id('availability'))

| Platform | Backend | Text | Images | Background reads |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Linux (Wayland) | `ext-data-control-v1` on workers, `GdkClipboard` on the main thread | ✓ | PNG | ✓ where the compositor offers `ext-data-control-v1`; else through GTK |
| Linux (X11) | `GdkClipboard` | ✓ | PNG (any format GDK decodes) | ✓ |
| Windows | Win32 `OpenClipboard`, `CF_UNICODETEXT`, registered `PNG` and `CF_DIB`/`CF_DIBV5` | ✓ | PNG; DIBs converted | ✓ |
| macOS | `NSPasteboard` (general pasteboard) | ✓ | PNG; TIFF converted | ✓ |
| Android | `ClipboardManager` | ✓ | first image `Uri` of the clip, re-encoded as PNG | ✗: Android 10+ returns `""` to apps not in focus |
| iOS | `UIPasteboard` (general pasteboard) | ✓ | PNG; JPEG, HEIC... converted | ✓, with iOS's "pasted from" banner |

The module is **on by default** everywhere (`clipboard` in
`b.dependency("oriel", .{ ... })`; `.clipboard = false` leaves it out).
`oriel.clipboard.available` is `true` whenever it's built, and
`window.oriel.features.clipboard` tells the page. It needs no permission
on any platform; iOS 16+ may ask the user when the app reads what another
app copied.

# [Quick start]($heading.id('quick-start'))

```zig
// build.zig: on by default; shown here to be explicit
const dep = b.dependency("oriel", .{ .target = target, .optimize = optimize, .clipboard = true });
```

```zig
// src/main.zig
const std = @import("std");
const oriel = @import("oriel");
const app = @import("oriel_app");

pub const Commands = struct {
    /// Reads may wait for another app: run this command on a worker.
    pub const async_commands = .{"paste"};

    pub fn copy(_: std.mem.Allocator, args: struct { text: []const u8 }) !void {
        try oriel.clipboard.writeText(args.text);
    }

    pub fn paste(gpa: std.mem.Allocator) ![]const u8 {
        return oriel.clipboard.readText(gpa);
    }
};

pub const Events = struct {};

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    return oriel.main(init, .{ .commands = Commands, .events = Events }, .{
        .app = app.build,
        .window = .{ .title = "Clipboard", .width = 600, .height = 400 },
    });
}
```

```js
await window.oriel.invoke("copy", { text: "Hello from the page" });
const text = await window.oriel.invoke("paste", {});
```

# [How it works]($heading.id('how-it-works'))

>[]($block.attrs("mermaid"))
```
flowchart LR
  Page -->|"invoke('paste')"| Worker["Worker thread<br/>(async command, App.spawn)"]
  Worker -->|"readText (blocks)"| API["oriel.clipboard"]
  API -->|"Linux Wayland:<br/>ext-data-control<br/>(own connection)"| Compositor
  API -->|"everything else:<br/>marshal and wait"| Main["Main thread"]
  Main -->|"GdkClipboard / OpenClipboard /<br/>NSPasteboard / ClipboardManager /<br/>UIPasteboard"| OS["System clipboard"]
  Main -.->|"readTextAsync callback"| Zig["Main-thread code<br/>(hotkey, tray)"]
```

Every platform's clipboard belongs to the UI thread, so each backend runs
the actual clipboard call there:

- **Windows, macOS, Android, iOS** (`src/plugins/clipboard/windows.zig`,
  `macos.zig`, `android.zig`, `ios.zig`): a call made on a worker is
  marshalled to the main thread with `Shell.runOnMainThread` and the worker
  waits for it; a call made on the main thread runs there directly. The
  async reads are queued to the main thread with `Shell.dispatchWithCleanup`
  and call back there; if the app stops before the queued read runs, the
  callback gets `error.AppNotRunning`.
- **Linux** (`src/plugins/clipboard/linux.zig`): GTK can't serve its own
  selection while the main thread is blocked waiting for it, so a blocking
  read is never allowed on the main thread (`error.WouldBlockMainThread`).
  On a worker, under Wayland, the read goes through the
  `ext-data-control-v1` protocol on a private Wayland connection
  (`src/plugins/wayland_globals.zig`): it works without window focus. Without
  that protocol (X11, or a compositor that lacks it) the worker hands the
  read to the main loop's `GdkClipboard` and waits up to 5 s. Writes from
  a worker are handed to the main loop the same way.
- **Your own data.** Every Linux write also offers a per-process marker
  type (`application/x-oriel-owner-<pid>`) and keeps a copy; a read that
  sees the marker returns that copy without a round trip through the
  compositor.

Images are PNG on the Zig side everywhere. Windows reads the registered
`PNG` format first (trimmed past its `IEND` chunk, since `GlobalSize` pads
it), else converts `CF_DIBV5`/`CF_DIB` (24 or 32 bpp, top-down or
bottom-up, bitfields; at most 32768 px a side) to PNG; writes put both a
DIB and the PNG. macOS writes PNG and TIFF (what most Mac apps paste) and
converts TIFF on read. iOS converts any image UIKit reads. Android reads
the clip's first `image/*` `Uri` and re-encodes it, and writes the PNG to
the runtime's `FileProvider` and puts its `content://` URI on the clip.

# [Examples]($heading.id('examples'))

## [Copy from a global hotkey]($heading.id('example-hotkey'))

A [global shortcut]($link.page('docs/global-shortcuts')) callback runs on
the main thread. Writing there is fine; reading belongs on a worker:

```zig
fn onHotkey(_: []const u8) void {
    oriel.App.spawn(rewriteSelection, .{}) catch |err| std.log.err("spawn: {s}", .{@errorName(err)});
}

fn rewriteSelection() !void {
    const gpa = std.heap.smp_allocator;
    const text = try oriel.clipboard.readText(gpa); // worker: may block
    defer gpa.free(text);
    const upper = try std.ascii.allocUpperString(gpa, text);
    defer gpa.free(upper);
    try oriel.clipboard.writeText(upper);
}
```

## [Read without blocking the main thread]($heading.id('example-async'))

```zig
fn onTrayItem(_: []const u8, _: ?bool) void {
    oriel.clipboard.readTextAsync(onText, null);
}

fn onText(result: anyerror![]const u8, _: ?*anyopaque) void {
    const text = result catch |err| return std.log.warn("clipboard: {s}", .{@errorName(err)});
    // `text` is valid only during this call: copy it to keep it.
    std.log.info("clipboard has {d} bytes", .{text.len});
}
```

## [Save and restore what the user had]($heading.id('example-restore'))

GhostPen puts its result on the clipboard to paste it, then puts back what
was there:

```zig
const Saved = union(enum) { none, text: []u8, image: []u8 };

fn save(gpa: std.mem.Allocator) Saved {
    const image = oriel.clipboard.readImage(gpa) catch null;
    if (image) |png| return .{ .image = png };
    const text = oriel.clipboard.readText(gpa) catch return .none;
    return .{ .text = text };
}

fn restore(saved: Saved) void {
    switch (saved) {
        .none => {},
        .text => |t| oriel.clipboard.writeText(t) catch {},
        .image => |png| oriel.clipboard.writeImage(png) catch {},
    }
}
```

`examples/showcase` (System tab: copy, paste, dictate anywhere) and
`examples/smoke` (a write/read round trip) use the module too.

# [Reference]($heading.id('reference'))

## [Functions]($heading.id('functions'))

```zig
pub const available: bool; // true when the module is built

pub fn readText(gpa: std.mem.Allocator) ![]u8;
pub fn readImage(gpa: std.mem.Allocator) !?[]u8;
pub fn writeText(text: []const u8) !void;
pub fn writeImage(png_bytes: []const u8) !void;

pub const TextCallback = *const fn (result: anyerror![]const u8, user_data: ?*anyopaque) void;
pub const ImageCallback = *const fn (result: anyerror!?[]const u8, user_data: ?*anyopaque) void;
pub fn readTextAsync(callback: TextCallback, user_data: ?*anyopaque) void;
pub fn readImageAsync(callback: ImageCallback, user_data: ?*anyopaque) void;

pub fn check(gpa: std.mem.Allocator, ctx: oriel.CheckContext) !oriel.Check;
```

| Function | Returns | Threads |
|---|---|---|
| `readText` | The clipboard's text, owned by the caller (`gpa`). `""` when it holds no text (or, on Android, when the app isn't focused). | Any on Windows, macOS, Android and iOS. **Linux: workers only**; the main thread gets `error.WouldBlockMainThread`. |
| `readImage` | PNG bytes owned by the caller, or `null` when there is no image. | As `readText`. |
| `writeText` | | Any thread. From a worker it waits for the main thread. |
| `writeImage` | | Any thread. The input must be an image the platform decodes: PNG on Android (checked by signature) and Windows (decoded with zigimg); anything GDK, AppKit or UIKit reads on Linux, macOS and iOS. Otherwise `error.InvalidImage` (Linux: `error.TextureNew`). |
| `readTextAsync` | Calls `callback` on the main thread with the text, valid only during the call. | Linux: call it on the main thread. Elsewhere any thread. |
| `readImageAsync` | Calls `callback` with PNG bytes or `null`, valid only during the call. | As `readTextAsync`. |
| `check` | The module's smoke check (`oriel.checkAll`): which backend is usable. macOS runs a text and PNG round trip on a private pasteboard; Android replaces the user's clipboard with a test string. | |

The clipboard holds one thing at a time: writing text drops an image and
the other way round. Text is UTF-8 on the Zig side (UTF-16 on Windows,
converted).

**Android focus.** Android 10 and later only give the clipboard to the app
in focus. A read on a worker waits up to 1.5 s for one of the app's windows
to get focus (so a read made as a window opens sees the clipboard); a read
on the UI thread can't wait and reads at once.

**Limits.** A Wayland background read gives up when the owner sends
nothing for 2 s (`error.ClipboardTimeout`) or more than 256 MiB
(`error.ClipboardTooLarge`); a worker waiting for the Linux main loop gives
up after 5 s (`error.MainThreadTimeout`). Windows retries `OpenClipboard`
10 times, 10 ms apart, while another app holds it.

## [Errors]($heading.id('errors'))

| Error | Where | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `error.WouldBlockMainThread` | Linux | `readText` / `readImage` on the main thread: use a worker or the async reads |
| `error.MainThreadTimeout` | Linux | a worker waited 5 s for the main loop (it isn't running, or another app didn't answer) |
| `error.ClipboardTimeout`, `error.ClipboardTooLarge` | Linux (Wayland) | the selection owner was too slow, or sent too much |
| `error.NoDisplay` | Linux | no GDK display (GTK not initialized) |
| `error.ClipboardReadFailed`, `error.ClipboardSetFailed`, `error.TextureNew` | Linux | GDK refused the read or write, or couldn't decode the image (logged) |
| `error.AppNotRunning` | Windows, macOS, Android, iOS | called before the app's main loop started, or after it stopped (call from `setup` or later) |
| `error.ClipboardOpenFailed` | Windows | another app kept the clipboard open |
| `error.ClipboardWriteFailed` | macOS, Android | the pasteboard or `ClipboardManager` refused the write |
| `error.InvalidImage` | Windows, macOS, Android, iOS | `writeImage` got bytes that aren't a readable image, or a read image couldn't be converted |
| `error.InvalidUtf8` | macOS, iOS | `writeText` got invalid UTF-8 |

## [Migrating from 0.11]($heading.id('migrating'))

- `oriel.clipboard.common` and `impl` are no longer public.

# [Troubleshooting]($heading.id('troubleshooting'))

- **`error.WouldBlockMainThread` on Linux.** The read ran on the main
  thread: a sync command, a hotkey or tray callback, `setup`. List the
  command in `async_commands`, or `oriel.App.spawn` the work, or use
  `readTextAsync`.
- **Reads return stale or empty text on Wayland while the window is in the
  background.** The compositor doesn't offer `ext-data-control-v1`; the
  smoke check (`oriel.checkAll`) then reports `GdkClipboard on the app's
  display (Wayland data-control: none)`. The older
  `zwlr_data_control_manager_v1` is detected but not used.
- **Android reads return `""`.** The app has no focused window (Android
  10+). Read in response to the user, while the window is in front.
- **iOS shows "pasted from" every time.** That is iOS's behaviour for any
  read of another app's content; read only when the user asks.

